LOCATION:560 Trinity Ave\, University City\, 63130
SUMMARY:The Voice of the Piano Informance
CLASS:PUBLIC
DESCRIPTION:Tickets\n[https://www.zeffy.com/en-US/ticketing/the-voice-of-th
 e-piano-informance?zlinkid=2775e3ec-9494-4855-acf2-73b72618f07f]\n$15 - Wa
 shU Students\n$35 -Regular Admission\n \n6:30 PM Pre-Concert Talk\n7:00 P
 M Voicing Demonstration\n \nThe Piano Technicians Guild Midwest Regional 
 Conference is presenting\na masterclass/concert on voicing a concert grand
  piano. Join our\nresident Great Artists Series Concert Piano Technician\,
  Keith\nWehmeier\, and Registered Piano Technician Ben Cross in the E. Des
 mond\nLee Concert Hall as they demonstrate on one of our Steinway D concer
 t\ngrands everything that goes into curating the singing tone of the\npian
 o before a concert!\n \nIn preparation for our extra special guest\, Yuja
  Wang\, Keith spent the\nsummer reconditioning the action parts and instal
 ling new hammers on\none of our concert grands.  The piano has since been
  regulated to\nplay like new and now awaits the exciting task of voicing t
 he hammers\nto really put the finishing touches on the piano.\n \nCome an
 d witness this once-in-a-lifetime event and hear what is\npossible for you
 r own instruments at home as well!\n\n \n\n \n
DTSTAMP:20261119T182346Z
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:17034
DTSTART;TZID=America/Chicago:20261016T150000
SEQUENCE:0
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
DTEND;TZID=America/Chicago:20261016T160000
SUMMARY:Department of Music Lecture: Graduate Student Conference Lectures
CLASS:PUBLIC
DESCRIPTION:LECTURER\n\nJ. P. Cupps\, PhD in Music Theory at Washington Uni
 versity in St. Louis\n\nTITLE\n\nAn Unknown Infinite (Archive): Flow\, Lay
 ering\, and Rupture as a\nFugitive Theory for Hip-Hop Form\n\nABSTRACT\n\n
 Responding to calls to deconstruct the mandate of legibility and\npolitics
  of racial exclusion inscribed in hip-hop formal analysis\,\nthis paper re
 visits the culture-theoretic tools developed by\nethnomusicologist Tricia 
 Rose as the foundation for a theory of form\nin hip-hop production. I argu
 e that – despite Rose’s canonic\nreception in the fields of hip-hop st
 udies\, cultural studies\, and\nother related fields – she has been rend
 ered peripheral to music\ntheory on account of its epistemologically white
 -and-male frame. She\nthus represents what Marc Hannaford has recently def
 ined as a fugitive\nmusic theorist\; more specifically\, her conception of
  flow\, layering\,\nand ruptures in line provides a basis for analyzing hi
 p-hop’s\nmusical forms.\n\nTo consider these concepts as fugitive music 
 theory\, I demonstrate how\nthey operate in an alternative theoretical spa
 ce to popular music\ntheory’s governing precepts of function\, hierarchy
 \, and syntax. I\ncontend that Rose embeds the musical within the visual\,
 \nconceptualizing hip-hop’s process of musical assembly in relation to\n
 fields like film editing and visual collage. I corroborate by\nexploring o
 verlaps with visual art theory and practice presented by\nthe Black cinema
 tographer Arthur Jafa\, to whom Rose attributes her\ndevelopment of the co
 ncepts\, and to visual collage practice of Romare\nBearden. My analysis th
 en turns to visual and sonic objects from\nQueens hip-hop artists amani an
 d King Vision Ultra’s LP _An Unknown\nInfinite_. Examining both the visu
 al elements of collage demonstrated\nin the album’s “archive book” a
 nd their sonic counterpart\ndemonstrated on the track “Scrapes\,” I co
 nclude that flow\,\nlayering\, and rupture relationships expressed in thes
 e texts are\nirreducible from formal analysis\, and contrary to pop-rock t
 heory\,\nremain embedded within hip-hop as a set of intermedial and\ninter
 cultural practices.\n\nBIOGRAPHY\n\nJ. P. Cupps is a PhD candidate in musi
 c theory and Lynne Cooper Harvey\nfellow in American Culture Studies. Thei
 r research centers on race\,\nregionalism\, and recording process in music
 al subcultures across the\nUS\, spanning interregional scenes for electron
 ic and dance music\, as\nwell as styles within broad umbrellas of post-pun
 k and hardcore. This\npaper’s materials are drawn from their dissertatio
 n\, tentatively\ntitled _An Unknown Infinite: Musical Practices and Discou
 rses of\nUndergroundness in Contemporary Hip-Hop._ In this project\, J. P.
 \ncollaborates with and analyzes alongside members of the Queens-based\nhi
 p-hop crew PTP to consider how these artists express\, historicize\,\nand 
 revise the larger imaginary of the hip-hop tradition within the\nflexible 
 boundaries of the contemporary New York DIY music scene.\n\n--------------
 -----------\n\n  \n\nLECTURER\n\nChristina Smiley\, PhD student in Musico
 logy at Washington University\nin St. Louis\n\nTITLE\n\n(Sonic) Environmen
 ts: Black Ecological Activism in Wadada Leo\nSmith’s America’s Nationa
 l Parks (2016)\n\nABSTRACT\n\nThere is a myth that African Americans\, bro
 adly speaking\, are detached\nfrom the environment—that Blackness and na
 ture\, and by extension\nenvironmentalism\, are incommensurate concepts. T
 aking up an invocation\nto narrate Black ecological engagement and activis
 m\, I engage with\nAACM composer and trumpeter Wadada Leo Smith and his 20
 16 album\n_America’s National Parks_\, to consider how Black musicians e
 ngage\n(with) the environment. Smith’s album attempts to narrate a holis
 tic\naccount of African American’s relationship to waterways\, land\, an
 d\nthe environment broadly. Various movements from the work serve as\nconc
 eptual points of departure for dealing with the fraught histories\nof Blac
 k connection to United States’ soil from the brutal and\nexploitative hi
 stories of enslavement\, sharecropping\, and lynching to\ncommunal healing
  and solidarities in the convergence of Black and\nIndigenous epistemologi
 es. Building on the work of scholars such as\nKimberly Ruffin\, Camille T.
  Dungy\, Dianne Glave\, and Michael W.\nMurphy\, my paper places Smith wit
 hin a genealogy of Black ecological\nactivists. I argue\, two-fold\, that 
 Black activists are not solely\ninterested in socio-political life\, but t
 hat their political\ncommitments extend to the environment. Conversely\, I
  suggest that\necological concerns are racial concerns\, impacting the his
 tories and\nlived experiences of African Americans. \n\nBIOGRAPHY\n\nChri
 stina Smiley is a 5th-year PhD candidate in musicology with an\nAmerican C
 ulture Studies certificate at Washington University in St.\nLouis. She is 
 a recipient of the American Musicological Society Howard\nMayer Brown fell
 owship\, the WashU CRE2 Race &amp; Ethnicity fellowship\,\nand the Lynne Coope
 r Harvey fellowships. Her dissertation “Beyond\nSignifyin’: Sound Expl
 orations in Black Studies and the Political\nPotential of Sacred Choral Wo
 rks (1900-1990)” explores Black\nparticipation in classical music\, cent
 ering the intersections of race\,\ntheology\, and politics in 20th-century
  large-scale sacred choral\nworks. Most recently\, she has presented at th
 e University of Mannheim\,\nthe American Musicological Society\, Indiana U
 niversity’s Jacobs\nSchool of Music\, Evangelisch Reformierte Kirche in 
 Leipzig\, Germany\,\nand performed as a soloist alongside Metropolitan ope
 ra singer\nAdrienne Danrich at the Sheldon Theatre. In conjunction with he
 r\nscholarly pursuits\, Christina is passionate about performance and\npub
 lic-facing education initiatives\, having worked closely with the\nSt. Lou
 is Children’s Choirs and the Opera Theatre of St. Louis\,\npresenting fo
 r the St. Louis Public School’s Teacher Professional\nDevelopment Works
 hop and the Engaging the Local Public Humanities\nColloquium and Workshop\
 , and serving as co-chair for the Julia Perry\nCentenary Symposium in 2024
 . \n
DTSTAMP:20261119T182346Z
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:17035
DTSTART;TZID=America/Chicago:20261030T150000
SEQUENCE:0
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
DTEND;TZID=America/Chicago:20261030T160000
SUMMARY:Department of Music Lecture: Graduate Student Conference Lectures
CLASS:PUBLIC
DESCRIPTION:LECTURER\n\nTad Biggs\, Ph.D. in Musicology at Washington Unive
 rsity in St. Louis\n\nTITLE\n\nA Fugitive Quixote: Julia Perry's _Quinary 
 Quixotic Songs_ (1976)\n\nABSTRACT\n\nCompleted in 1976 but unperformed a
 nd unpublished until 2024\, _Quinary\nQuixotic Songs_ stands out among Ju
 lia Perry’s final works and\nremains one of her least examined. Scored f
 or bass-baritone and an\nunusual chamber ensemble of flute\, clarinet\, vi
 ola\, baritone horn\, and\npiano\, the five-movement song cycle sets Perry
 ’s own surreal\,\ncryptic texts. Although the title invokes Cervantes’
  _Don Quixote_\,\nthe music stages a darker meditation on striving against
  injustice and\nhuman limitation\, locating a “quixotic” impulse in si
 tes as varied\nas an imprisoned poet and the spectacle of a bullfight.\n\n
 This paper argues that _Quinary Quixotic Songs_ offers unique insight\nin
 to ongoing historiographic efforts to categorize Perry within\nneoclassici
 st\, modernist\, and postmodernist frameworks. This study\nengages Tammy K
 ernodle’s account of the “Black postmodern concert\naesthetic” along
 side Ryan Dohoney’s notion of “metamodernist\ndrift\,” both of which
  advance historiographic and ideological\naccounts of Julia Perry’s musi
 cal aesthetic. By focusing on one of\nPerry’s later\, most experimental 
 works\, this paper extends and\nrecalibrates their insights. Through close
  analysis\, I demonstrate\nthat although Perry deploys postmodern composit
 ional techniques\, like\nfragmentation\, irony\, and intertextuality\, the
  work does not rupture\ninto a play of surfaces or postmodern detachment. 
 Each song\narticulates its own musico-structural logic and moves through\n
 dramatically different subjects\, generating a dizzying heterogeneity.\nFr
 agmentation becomes the condition through which structural depth\nemerges.
  Beneath this fractured surface persists a fundamentally\nmodernist utopia
 n gesture: an ethical demand for striving.\n\n_Quinary Quixotic Songs_ of
 fers a striking example of how Perry\noperated within a space of fugitive 
 abstraction\, where the work’s\nopacity unsettles hermeneutic containmen
 t yet betrays a distinctly\nmodernist meditation on striving as a necessar
 y\, if unrealizable\,\nutopian demand. Given Perry’s eclectic style\, th
 is work provides one\ninstance of how Perry’s music resists totalizing n
 arratives of race\,\ngender\, and conventional stylistic periodization in 
 twentieth-century\nAmerican concert music. This analysis thus contributes 
 to the\nnecessary recontextualization of Perry within ongoing historiograp
 hic\ndebates demanded by her diverse output and suggests a model for the\n
 interpretation of Perry and other Black women composers of her\ngeneration
 .\n\nBIOGRAPHY\n\nTad is a Ph.D. candidate in Musicology at Washington Uni
 versity in St.\nLouis\, where he is also a Lynne Cooper Harvey Fellowship 
 in American\nCulture Studies. \n\nTad’s research focuses on classical a
 nd popular music in the United\nStates\, with particular attention to Afri
 can American composers and\nthe ways race\, gender\, and class shape the r
 eception\, circulation\, and\nstudy of music. A central dimension of his w
 ork is the recovery of the\nlife and music of composer Julia Perry (1924
 –1979). As part of this\nproject\, Tad organized and secured funding for
  the 2024 Julia Perry\nCentenary Symposium and Concert\, a multi-day event
  in St. Louis that\nfeatured talks by an international group of guest scho
 lars\, a\nroundtable\, and a concert devoted entirely to Perry’s music.
  \n\nHis dissertation\, _Listening for Julia Perry’s Voice_\, traces a\
 nselection of her vocal works across the postwar era\, the Civil Rights\nM
 ovement\, and the Cold War\, situating her output within Black\nModernist 
 and feminist frameworks. The project blends musical analysis\nand archival
  research and extends critical theoretical approaches\nwithin the study of
  Black modernist concert music. \n\n-------------------------\n\nLECTURER
  \n\nKimia Fakharinia\, Ph.D. in Musicology at Washington University in S
 t.\nLouis\n\nTITLE\n\n“Too virile”: Progressive Compositional Ambition
  and Unconstrained\nVirtuosity in Cécile Chaminade’s Concertstück\n\nA
 BSTRACT\n\nCécile Chaminade was a French composer and pianist best known 
 for her\ncharacter pieces and mélodies. Yet her early oeuvre also include
 d\norchestral and symphonic works\, such as the _Concertstück_ for piano
 \nand orchestra. By the 1890s\, however\, her output shifted significantly
 \ntoward shorter\, commercially viable piano pieces and songs. Previous\ns
 cholarship\, notably Marcia J. Citron’s monograph\, has explained\nChami
 nade’s limited engagement with large-scale genres in financial\,\naesthe
 tic\, or discriminatory terms. While these factors form an\nimportant back
 drop to her career\, they leave room for closer\nanalytical attention to i
 ndividual large-scale works. I therefore\ncenter the _Concertstück_\, a c
 oncerted orchestral work through which\nChaminade staged her early artisti
 c ambitions\, yet one that has\nreceived little sustained scholarly analys
 is. Rather than focusing\nsolely on reception or scale\, I examine how Cha
 minade articulated her\nambitions within the composition itself\, engaging
  contemporary\naesthetics and culturally coded virtuosity in tension with 
 gendered\nexpectations in nineteenth-century musical discourse.\n\nI argue
  that the _Concertstück_ synthesizes two modes of\ncompositional and pia
 nistic performance: a compositional ambition\narticulated through Lisztian
  piano virtuosity\, rich orchestration\, and\nambiguous formal design alig
 ned with the “progressive” aesthetics\nof the New Germans\, and a high
 ly popular\, exoticist\, and culturally\ncoded mode of virtuosity in its i
 nvocation of the _style hongrois_. In\ndoing so\, Chaminade positions hers
 elf both as a composer of\nlarge-scale works and as a pianist capable of d
 elivering the\nexpressive intensity associated with the _style hongrois_. 
 Drawing on\nJonathan Bellman’s discussion of the _style_ _hongrois_ an
 d his\nexploration of nineteenth-century stereotypes of Roma\, I show that
 \nthis idiom carried connotations sharply opposed to the ideals of\ncultiv
 ation\, restraint\, and refinement that governed women’s musical\nexpres
 sion at the time. The reception of the _Concertstück _was\ngendered\, wi
 th a critic describing it as “strong and\nvirile—perhaps too virile\,
 ” lamenting the absence of the grace\ntypically associated with her pian
 o works and songs. The charge of\nvirility thus emerges as a response to C
 haminade’s aesthetic\ntransgression of prevailing norms of feminine musi
 cianship. This\ntension had lasting consequences\, contributing to her sub
 sequent move\naway from large-scale works in her compositional output and 
 concert\nprogramming.\n\nBIOGRAPHY\n\nKimia is a third-year PhD student in
  musicology. Her research centers\non nineteenth-century women composers a
 nd pianists such as Cécile\nChaminade and Fannie Bloomfield-Zeisler. Prio
 r to graduate studies at\nWashington University in St. Louis\, Kimia earne
 d her master’s in\npiano performance at the University of Tehran.\n\n---
 ----------------------\n\nLECTURER \n\nSeoyoon Choi\, Ph.D. in Music Theo
 ry at Washington University in St.\nLouis\n\nTITLE\n\n\"A New Phase of Sou
 th Korean Democratic Participation: K-pop Fandom\,\nYoung Women\, and the 
 Transformation of Protest Culture\"\n\nABSTRACT\n\nIn December 2024\, Sout
 h Korean citizens organized large-scale yet\npeaceful rallies demanding th
 en-President Yoon’s resignation after\nhis declaration of martial law. M
 usic played a visible role in the\nrallies\, from collective singing to th
 e use of K-pop light sticks and\nthe recirculation of “Into the New Worl
 d” (2007)\, the debut song\nof the prominent K-pop girl group Girls’ G
 eneration. In this paper\,\nI argue that K-pop and its largely female fand
 om practices have\ntransformed South Korean protest culture by translating
  fan-based\nsolidarity into political solidarity. I analyze how “Into th
 e New\nWorld” came to function as a protest anthem and how fan-based\ncu
 ltural practices and responses to women’s political expression\nfacilita
 ted new forms of participation in protest movements.\n\nI demonstrate how 
 contemporary protest practices differ from those of\nthe Gwangju Uprising 
 of 1980\, a response to the military coup and\nmartial law under Chun Doo-
 hwan. Citizens resisted state violence\nthrough armed struggle and protest
  songs\, including the national\nanthem and “March for Our Beloved” (1
 981). Traumatic memories of\nthis period have shaped later protest practic
 es: rather than\nconfronting authorities through physical conflict\, conte
 mporary\nprotesters emphasize solidarity through collective\, nonviolent\n
 expression.\n\nIn the 2016 and 2024 impeachment rallies\, “Into the New 
 World”\nemerged as a powerful protest anthem. Its lyrics such as “Migh
 t be\nan unknown future with obstacles\, but I won’t change” and “We
 \ncan’t give up” were recontextualized as protest slogans\, while\nK-p
 op light sticks\, originally designed for concerts\, were repurposed\nas v
 isual symbols of collective presence. Young women with experience\nof affe
 ctive solidarity within K-pop fandoms played a central role in\ngenerating
  political-affective solidarity across diverse backgrounds\nand sustaining
  mass participation. In addition to fan-based practices\,\nwomen’s democ
 ratic engagement took shape under constraints on their\npolitical expressi
 on\, as evidenced by the cyberbullying of female\nsingers who implied poli
 tical solidarity with protesters through\nfan-communication platforms. Thi
 s study shows how “Into the New\nWorld” and the pressures surrounding 
 women’s political expression\njointly reshaped democratic participation 
 in South Korea\, signaling a\nnew phase in its ongoing evolution.\n\nBIOGR
 APHY\n\nSeoyoon was an active clarinetist who performed professionally\nth
 roughout her undergraduate studies. She then shifted her focus from\nperfo
 rmance to analysis and earned her master’s degree in Music\nTheory at Yo
 nsei University. For her master’s thesis\, she analyzed\nthe first movem
 ent of Brahms’s _Clarinet Sonata_ Op. 120/1 from\nSchenkerian perspecti
 ve\, focusing on its non-stereotypical scheme. She\nis currently working o
 n Debussy’s_ Première rhapsodie_ and\nexpanding her research on K-pop
  and its political and social\ninfluences.\n
DTSTAMP:20261119T182346Z
END:VEVENT
