BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:17003
DTSTART;TZID=America/Chicago:20261031T150000
SEQUENCE:0
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
DTEND;TZID=America/Chicago:20261031T160000
LOCATION:560 Trinity Ave\, University City\, 63130
SUMMARY:Department of Music Showcase
CLASS:PUBLIC
DESCRIPTION:Spend an afternoon enjoying the musical talents of students fro
 m all\nover Washington University. The Music Department Showcase is an\nex
 citing event featuring some of the University's most gifted singers\nand i
 nstrumentalists as soloists and in small ensembles. Free\nadmission. No re
 servations\, tickets\, or registration required. Free\nParking is availabl
 e in the garage behind the building.\n\nPROGRAM\n\nSonata in E minor\, Hob
  XVI:34 (1778) by Joseph Haydn (1732 - 1809)\n    _ Presto      
 _                    \nHayden McAllister\, piano\n\n_Vo
 i che sapete_ from _Le Nozze di Figaro_ (1786) by W.A. Mozart\n(1756 - 17
 91)\nAllison Cooper\, soprano\nYeon-Kyung Kim\, piano\n\n_Koyunbaba_ by Ca
 rlo Domeniconi (b. 1947)\n     _IV: Presto    _\n         
               \nDani Bosci\, guitar\n\n_Etude de Concert_ 
 “Au matin” (1940) by Marcel Tournier (1879 -\n1951)\nAmelia Schwartz\,
  harp\n\nConcerto in C Major\, K. 314 (1777) by W.A. Mozart\n     _Alle
 gro Aperto_\nMichelle Zhang\, oboe\nYeon-Kyung Kim\, piano\n\n_Deluge_ by 
 Wayne Shorter (1933 - 2023)\nPhil Zhang\, guitar\nLandon Kahl\, guitar\n\n
 _O mio babbino caro_ from _Gianni Schicchi _(1917) by Giacomo Puccini\n(1
 858 - 1924)\nJuliana Cool\, soprano\nYeon-Kyung Kim\, piano\n\nString Quar
 tet No. 1 in G minor (1877) by Edvard Grieg (1843 - 1907)\n     _Un poc
 o andante-Allegro molto ed agitato    _\n          \nBradley
  Gober\, violin  \nMichelle Lu\, violin  \nDelia Zacks\, viola\nColette 
 Wicks\, cello\n\n_Transcendental_ Etude No. 10 in F minor (1851) by Franz 
 Liszt (1811 -\n1886)\nJane Ser\, piano\n\n_Ugetsu _(1975) by Cedar Walton 
 (1934 - 2013)\n_Blue in Green_ (1959) by Bill Evans (1929 - 1980)\nMargo N
 euberger\, voice\nBinghan Yan\, guitar\nOwen Kim\, saxophone\nNate Sapp\, 
 piano\nGavin Goldsmith\, drums\n\nTRANSLATIONS\n\n_VOI CHE SAPETE_\nYou la
 dies\nWho know what love is\,\nSee if it is\nWhat I have in my heart.\nAll
  that I feel\nI will explain\;\nSince it is new to me\,\nI don't understan
 d it.\nI have a feeling\nFull of desire\,\nWhich now is pleasure\,\nNow is
  torment.\nI freeze\, then I feel\nMy spirit all ablaze\,\nAnd the next mo
 ment\nTurn again to ice.\nI seek for a treasure\nOutside of myself\;\nI kn
 ow not who holds it\nNor what it is.\nI sigh and I groan\nWithout wishing 
 to\,\nI flutter and tremble\nWithout knowing why.\nI find no peace\nBy nig
 ht or day\,\nBut yet to languish thus\nIs sheer delight.\nYou ladies\nWho 
 know what love is\,\nSee if it is\nWhat I have in my heart.\n\n_O MIO BABB
 INO CARO_\nOh my dear papa\,\nI love him\, he is handsome\, handsome.\nI w
 ant to go to Porta Rossa\nTo buy the ring!\nYes\, yes\, I want to go there
 !\nAnd if I loved him in vain\,\nI would go to the Ponte Vecchio\,\nbut to
  throw myself in the Arno!\nI am anguished and tormented!\nOh God\, I'd wa
 nt to die!\nPapa\, have pity\, have pity!\nPapa\, have pity\, have pity!\n
DTSTAMP:20261119T182346Z
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:16995
DTSTART;TZID=America/Chicago:20260918T193000
SEQUENCE:0
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
DTEND;TZID=America/Chicago:20260918T203000
SUMMARY:\"Music to heal the soul\" featuring Ustad Shafaat Khan\,\nsitar/vo
 cals/tabla
CLASS:PUBLIC
DESCRIPTION:Ustad Shafaat Khan will present an energetic\, engaging\, and\n
 educational performance featuring the rich traditions of Indian\nClassical
  and Folk music.\n\nIn addition to the performance\, he will guide audienc
 es through the\nhistory and cultural significance of Indian music\, introd
 ucing the\nconcepts of Raag\, Taal\, traditional instruments\, and perform
 ance\ntechniques. The program includes selections that showcase Indian\nCl
 assical music as well as lighter musical forms such as Qawwali\,\nGhazal\,
  and Thumri\, offering listeners a diverse and captivating\nmusical experi
 ence.\n\n&gt; \"Virtuoso...Classical purity astonishing... intellectual 
 and\n&gt; poetic...creator as well as performer\" - _THE WASHINGTON 
 POST_\n&gt; \n&gt; \"Absolutely superb\"\n&gt; _THE TIMES_ London\, England\
 n\nBIOGRAPHIES:\n\nUSTAD SHAFAAT KHAN\n\nUstad Shafaat Khan's career is no
 thing short of extraordinary. He\ndebuted at the Kings Lynn Festival opene
 d by the Queen of England. The\nson of Ustad Imrat Khan and nephew of Usta
 d Vilayat Khan\, he has\ngraced the stages of prestigious concert halls\, 
 festivals\, and\nuniversities across India\, the U.S.\, Europe\, China\, R
 ussia\, and Japan.\nHe has performed alongside legends like Stevie Wonder 
 at Bonnaroo and\nshared a televised concert with Ray Charles in Germany.\n
 \nDANIEL JOHNSON : TABLA/PERCUSSION/VOX\n\nDaniel’s path in Music has be
 en a journey from the pounding\npercussive energy of performing with Phila
 delphia’s 20 member Spoken\nHand Percussion Orchestra\, to the Freedom a
 nd Vibe of performing with\nMiles Davis alum’s Badal Roy’s “Dharma J
 azz” to the intense\ndepth of Tabla player to North Indian Classical Sit
 ar legend Ustad\nShafaat Khan. These and many more projects including bein
 g a\npercussionist at Princeton University for the department of Modern\nD
 ance have kept him moving further into subtle pulsation.\n\nANAWIM AVILA 
 - SAXOPHONE \n\nAnawim Avila is a multi-instrumentalist and composer. Hi
 s eclectic\ncareer includes collaborations with the Odean Pope Saxophone C
 hoir\,\nMaryland Symphony Orchestra\, Billy Harper\, Les Savy Fav\, and ma
 ny\nmore. He is currently active in the jazz fusion group Blás Vasco\,\n
 singer/songwriter Christina Ward’s band\, prog-rock outfit Clockwise\non
  Fire\, as well as his own art-dance-rock group\, Our Bodies\nThemselves.
  He studied composition under influential mentors Tania\nLeón\, Amir Sh
 pilman\, Charles Tolliver\, and Knoel Scott of the Sun Ra\nArkestra\, and 
 has had his pieces performed internationally.\n  \n\n \n
DTSTAMP:20261119T182346Z
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:17000
DTSTART;TZID=America/Chicago:20261021T193000
SEQUENCE:0
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
DTEND;TZID=America/Chicago:20261021T203000
LOCATION:560 Trinity Ave\, University City\, 63130
SUMMARY:WashU Jazz Series: Jazz Band Concert
CLASS:PUBLIC
DESCRIPTION:Get ready for a night of big band jazz! The WashU Jazz Band\, u
 nder the\ndirection of Chris Becker\, takes the stage with a fantastic lin
 eup\nfeaturing Hank Mobley’s _This I Dig of You\,_ Wayne Shorter’s\n_C
 hildren of the Night_\, the playful _Peel Me a Grape_\, and Ellen\nRowe’
 s _The Hip-Hop Bop Stop_. A special highlight is _Tribute to\nMiles_\, a m
 edley of Miles Davis classics including _So What\, All\nBlues_\, and _Mile
 stones_\, celebrating the centennial of his birth.\nWith great ensemble pl
 aying\, outstanding student soloists\, and plenty\nof jazz favorites\, thi
 s is an evening you won’t want to miss!\n\nWatch Live [https://youtube.c
 om/live/tBSZNGXBnb0?feature=share]\n\nPROGRAM\n\n_This I Dig of You_ (1960
 )\, Hank Mobley (1930-1986)\, arr. Mike Tomaro\n_Peel Me A Grape_ (1962)\,
  Dave Frishberg (1933-2021)\, arr. George\nStone\n_Children of the Night_ 
 (1961)\, Wayne Shorter (1933-2023)\, arr. Mark\nTaylor\n_All or Nothing at
  All_ (1939)\, Words by Jack Lawrence (1912-2009)\,\nMusic by Arthur Altma
 n (1910-1994)\, arr. George Stone\n_Tribute to Miles_\, arr. Mark Taylor -
  Medley of _So What_ (1959)\,\n_All Blues_ (1959)\, _Seven Steps to Heaven
  _(1963)\, and _Milestones_\n(1948)\, in recognition of the centennial of 
 Miles Davis' birth.\n_The Hip-Hop Bop Stop _(2017)\, Ellen Rowe (b. 1958)\
 n\nJAZZ BAND\n\nSAXOPHONES\nNoah Clayton\, Alto\nEthan Lin\, Alto\nAaron C
 iu\, Tenor\nOwen Ross\, Tenor\nSeth Steuck\, Baritone\n\nTRUMPETS\nTori Va
 squez\nTeo Giannini\nCaleb Kessler\nKelsey Conover\nJeremy Xu\n\nTROMBONES
 \nJayden Thankamani\nEllis Kopcho\nJacob Karni\nHenry Wasserman\, Bass\n\n
 RHYTHM SECTION\nCody Peck\, Guitar\nElla Heisel\, Guitar\nSeoha Kim\, Guit
 ar\nHenry Daut\, Piano\nJunxian Xu\, Piano\nLivy Best\, Bass\nMatt Anto\, 
 Bass\nCollin Lloyd\, Drums\nYJ Zhao\, Drums\nWilson Hou\, Vibraphone\n\nDI
 RECTOR\n\nChris Becker is the Director of the Wind Ensemble and the Jazz B
 ands\nat Washington University in St. Louis\, and serves as the director o
 f\nwinds and percussion. Mr. Becker graduated from the University of\nMiss
 ouri - Kansas City Conservatory of Music “With Distinction” in\n1977\,
  receiving the Bachelor of Music Education Degree\, and earned a\nMaster o
 f Arts in Education from Lindenwood University.\n\nMr. Becker has taught i
 nstrumental music in Missouri for 49 years from\nthe elementary to the uni
 versity level. In June 2017\, he retired as\nDirector of Bands at Parkway 
 South High School\, after serving for 36\nyears in the Parkway School Dist
 rict (St. Louis County\, Missouri).\nDuring his tenure there\, he directed
  Marching Bands\, Symphonic Bands\,\nJazz Bands\, and all other related ba
 nd activities.\n\nIn addition\, he is the Artistic Director and Conductor 
 of the\nManchester Community Band\, a highly successful ensemble founded t
 en\nyears ago and enthusiastically received by the residents of\nMancheste
 r. Mr. Becker has also been a frequent guest conductor for\nboth concert b
 ands and jazz bands in Missouri and Illinois.\n\nMr. Becker has served as 
 the Jazz Vice President for the Missouri\nMusic Educators Association\, th
 e first educator to hold that position\nafter it was established as a part
  of the MMEA Board. Mr. Becker is a\nPast President of the Missouri unit o
 f the International Association\nof Jazz Educators. During his term he hel
 ped clarify the mission and\ngoals of the unit\, and endeavored to reach o
 ut to music educators\,\nthose in the music industry\, and professional pl
 ayers to create a\nbroader base of support for jazz education. Becker has 
 also served as\nJazz Vice President for the St. Louis Suburban Music Educa
 tors\nAssociation. Becker plays the trumpet professionally with a variety 
 of\nensembles in the St. Louis area\, and has taught trumpet privately in\
 nboth the Kansas City and St. Louis areas.\n\nMr. Becker is a member of th
 e College Band Directors National\nAssociation\, the National Association 
 for Music Education\, the\nMissouri Music Educators Association\, the Miss
 ouri Association for\nJazz Education\, the Missouri Band Masters Associati
 on\, and Phi Beta Mu\nBand Directors Fraternity. He was named \"Charles Em
 mons Outstanding\nBand Director of the Year\" by Phi Beta Mu in January 19
 99. He was\nnominated numerous times for the Pillar of Parkway Award\, whi
 ch\nrecognizes commitment and dedication for work in the Parkway School\nD
 istrict\, and was awarded a Pillar of Parkway in April 2016. Mr.\nBecker w
 as honored for his distinguished career by the St. Louis\nSuburban Music E
 ducators Association in January 2018\, and named to\ntheir Hall of Fame.\n
DTSTAMP:20261119T182346Z
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:16993
DTSTART;TZID=America/Chicago:20260911T193000
SEQUENCE:0
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
DTEND;TZID=America/Chicago:20260911T203000
SUMMARY:Meridian Clarinet Quartet Presented by the Ariel Concert Series
CLASS:PUBLIC
DESCRIPTION:Performed by the Meridian Clarinet Quartet\, this program pairs
 \n19th-century Romantic works with 20th-century jazz and tango\ntraditions
 . It features chamber pieces by Mendelssohn and Tchaikovsky\nalongside sel
 ections by Gershwin and the quartet's own arrangement of\nPiazzolla.\n\nMU
 SICIANS\n\nSang Yoon Kim\, clarinet\nYao Guang Zhai\, clarinet​\nLin Ma\
 , clarinet\nTzuying Huang\, bass clarinet \n\nPROGRAM\n\nFelix Mendelssoh
 n: Concert Piece No. 1 in F Minor\, Op. 113\nFelix Mendelssohn: Concert Pi
 ece No. 2 in D Minor\, Op. 114\nGeorge Gershwin: Three Preludes\nPyotr Ily
 ich Tchaikovsky: Selections from The Seasons\nAstor Piazzolla: Nightclub 1
 960 for Clarinet Quintet (arr. Sang Yoon\nKim)\n
DTSTAMP:20261119T182346Z
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:16997
DTSTART;TZID=America/Chicago:20261013T193000
SEQUENCE:0
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
DTEND;TZID=America/Chicago:20261013T203000
LOCATION:560 Trinity Ave\, University City\, 63130
SUMMARY:WashU Jazz Series: Vincent Varvel Quartet
CLASS:PUBLIC
DESCRIPTION:Guitarist Vincent Varvel\, Director of Guitar Studies\, brings 
 his\nquartet to the 560 Music Center in the WashU Jazz Series.  The\nquar
 tet\, featuring WashU faculty Joel Vanderheyden on saxophone and\nelectron
 ics\, Eric Warren on bass\, Joe Meyer on drums\, and Varvel on\nelectric g
 uitars\, plays music that combines atmospheric electronica\,\ndeep grooves
 \, and captivating melodies with exploration and jazz\nimprovisation.\n
DTSTAMP:20261119T182346Z
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:16992
DTSTART;TZID=America/Chicago:20260908T193000
SEQUENCE:0
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
DTEND;TZID=America/Chicago:20260908T203000
SUMMARY:WashU Jazz Series: Carolbeth True-Two Times True
CLASS:PUBLIC
DESCRIPTION:Two Times True is an instrumental jazz and jazz/rock quartet th
 at\nformed in 2007\, thanks to our dear friend and former president of\nVi
 ctoria Records Bill Becker\, who liked the family connection within\nthe g
 roup. The instrumentation is Carolbeth True—piano and keys\;\nDave True
 —drums\; Glen Smith—bass\; and Larry Johnson—saxophones.\nThe group 
 has performed at many festivals and concerts in the area\,\nand is often j
 oined by Kim Fuller on vocals. Kim Fuller is a St. Louis\ntreasure\, havin
 g worked for many years with the Galaxy Band. Now Kim\nand the trio are al
 so members of The Oikos Ensemble\, and Kim and\nCarolbeth are members of t
 he cast of The Jazz Story—the original\neducational offering at The Shel
 don.\n\nBIOGRAPHIES\n\nPianist CAROLBETH TRUE has been a very active membe
 r of the St. Louis\nmusical community as a teacher/coach and performer. Sh
 e is a long-time\nmember of the Webster University music faculty\, also do
 ing extensive\nprivate teaching\; an original member of the cast of “The
  Jazz\nStory” at the Sheldon\, just finishing 27th season. She was also 
 the\npianist for the Missouri Music Program created by Paul Reuter\,\nform
 erly Sheldon executive director\, which presented\, around the\neastern pa
 rt of the state\, all styles of music composed and performed\nby Missouri 
 musicians. She was pianist with Variety Children’s\nTheatre doing all re
 hearsals and shows for 10 years\, as well as many\nyears with The Muny—r
 ehearsals and orchestra pianist. Her group Two\nTimes True\, with Dave Tru
 e on drums\, Larry Johnson—saxophones\, and\nGlen Smith—bass performs 
 around the St. Louis area in festivals and\nservices. The group is heard o
 n multiple recordings: “Two Times\nTrue” (2007)\, Larry Johnson’s 
 “Circles” and “I’m Just\nSayin’ “ and released “True Christm
 as”-- a cd of\njazz/contemporary settings of Christmas carols and hymns\
 , in 2018. The\nCarolbeth Trio\, with Glen Smith—bass and Kevin Gianino
 —drums is\nheard on the cd “True” as well as with vocalist Debby Len
 non on\nher cd “I’m All Smiles”. Carolbeth is a member of The Oikos\
 nEnsemble directed by saxophonist/ Reverend Cliff Aerie. She has backed\nm
 any name jazz artists\, performed a dozen concertos with various\norchestr
 as 1991-2005. Her awards include: Excellence in the Arts Award\nfrom Arts 
 and Education Council\; Riverfront Times Jazz Musician of the\nYear\; St. 
 Louis Musician of the Year by St. Louis Magazine\; Rock Hill\nCitizen of t
 he Year. Carolbeth is in her 13th year as rehearsal and\nperformance piani
 st for the MCDA Jazz Vocal Ensemble\, having worked\nwith top world-renown
 ed jazz vocal names including this past year’s\ndirector and clinician M
 ike Engelhardt and this year’s\, Janice\nVlachos.\n\nDAVE TRUE started p
 laying the drums at the age of two. He studied\ndrums with Kevin Gianino a
 nd classical percussion with Chris Treloar\nthroughout his school years in
  St. Louis. Dave performed with the\nParkway Central Marching Colts in the
  Rose Bowl Parade and the\nMacy’s Day Parade. As a percussionist\, David
  performed with the\nWashington University Wind Ensemble\, under the direc
 tion of Dan\nPresgrave. As a drum set artist\, David was involved in the\n
 All-Suburban jazz band and also performed with his own group\, formerly\nk
 nown as the TrueJazz Quintet. Before leaving St. Louis\, Dave received\nth
 e prestigious Louis Armstrong Jazz Award. \n\nDave True continued his own
  studies at DePaul University in Chicago\,\nreceiving a music business deg
 ree. Upon returning to St. Louis\, he\nbegan working for Commerce Bank and
  was with that organization for 15\nyears in multiple capacities. Recently
 \, Dave has developed his own\ncompany: Shield Pro Home Services\, where h
 e offers his skills in\nmaintenance and repair. Musically\, he plays with 
 the stellar party\nband Fanfare\, the Concordia Kirkwood Praise Band\, the
  Oikos Ensemble\,\nand of course Two Times True. He and his wife Kelly hav
 e two wonderful\nchildren: Ava and Logan.\n\nGLEN SMITH was born into a ho
 me where music was always playing\, and\neveryone in the family played an 
 instrument.  Glen took up the guitar\nwhen he was 12 years old and shortl
 y thereafter was performing at\nparties and dances in a band with his Fath
 er.  Glen went to college\nto study music theory and classical guitar bef
 ore leaving to travel\nwith a number of national touring acts.  Glen retu
 rned to St. Louis\,\nwhere he spent years as an A-List bass player\, worki
 ng shows for star\nperformers at the Fox Theatre\, Powell Hall\, and other
  venues.  \n\nGlen is humbled to have been able to play with some of the 
 finest\nmusicians in the world who live and work right here in St. Louis\,
 \nincluding the pianist Carolbeth True\, guitarist Eric Slaughter\,\ntrump
 eter Jim Manley\, and many others.\n\nLARRY JOHNSON is a highly regarded s
 axophone player and mentor. In the\narea of education\, he won the Outstan
 ding Teacher Award from the\nArchdiocese in 2004\; has been nominated eigh
 t times for “Who’s Who\nAmong American Teachers”\; is band director 
 at St. Joseph’s School\nin Cottleville and St. Dominic High School jazz 
 band director. \n\nLarry has been a professional musician for 35 years\, 
 his longest\nassociation being with Jim Manley’s groups Fantasy and Wild
  Cool and\nSwingin’\; and Charley Boehme’s Charley B Jazz. He has perf
 ormed\nwith Two Times True\, now in its 19th year\, since 2007. Larry is o
 n the\n2007 cd TWO TIMES TRUE\; and his own releases CIRCLES and I’M JUS
 T\nSAYIN.\n
DTSTAMP:20261119T182346Z
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:17007
DTSTART;TZID=America/Chicago:20261115T150000
SEQUENCE:0
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
DTEND;TZID=America/Chicago:20261115T160000
SUMMARY:WashU Choirs Concert: Seeking Light
CLASS:PUBLIC
DESCRIPTION:What does it mean to persevere through the darkness - and what 
 happens\nwhen we finally find the light?\n\nJoin the WashU Chamber Choir a
 nd Concert Choir for a powerful musical\njourney exploring resilience\, ho
 pe\, and the emotional landscape of\ndifficult times. Featuring W.A. Mozar
 t’s _Sancta Maria\, Mater Dei_\,\nOla Gjeilo’s _Dark Night of the Soul
 _\, and more.\n\nCome experience an evening of music that reminds us that 
 even in the\ndarkest moments\, there is always a path toward light.\n\nDig
 ital Program\n[https://wustl.box.com/s/30v4q6v8flxis3d1do2ladpxgnkiwtyv]\n
 \nWatch Live [https://youtube.com/live/_nrQylQzER8?feature=share]\n\nPROGR
 AM\n\nCHAMBER CHOIR\n_By Night_ (2023) by Elaine Hagenberg (b. 1979) Word
 s by Harriet\nPrescott Spofford (1835 - 1921)\n     Claire Yi\, violin\
 n     Evelyn Nedelciuc\, cello\n     Ben Dicke\, percussion\n_Noctur
 ne_ (1994) by Adolphus Hailstork (b. 1941) Words by Rev. Jim\nCurtis (1921
  - 2021)\n_The Sun Never Says_ (2018) by Dan Forrest (b. 1978) Words by Da
 niel\nLadinsky (b. 1948)\n     Evelyn Nedelciuc\, cello\n_The May Night
 _ (1962) by Johannes Brahms (1833 – 1897) Arr. Arthur\nFrackenpohl (1924
  – 2019)\n_Grace Before Sleep_ (2011) by Susan LaBarr (b. 1981) Words by
  Sara\nTeasdale (1884 -1933)\n_RIUH_ (2024) by Tracy Wong (b.1985) Words b
 y Bihzhu\n     Crystal Lovato\, soloist\n     Wilson Hou\, vocal per
 cussion\n\nCONCERT CHOIR\n_Hymn to the Sun _(_Hymne au Soleil_) (1918) by 
 Lili Boulanger (1893 -\n1918)\n     Ava Ayad\, soloist\n_Sancta Maria_\
 , K 273 (1777) by W.A. Mozart (1756 - 1791)\n_Holding the Light_ (2013) by
  B.E. Boykin (b.1989) Words by Stuart\nKestenbaum\n_Seeking Light_ (2020) 
 by Brandon Williams\, Words by Charles Anthony\nSilvestri (b. 1965)\n  
    Danae Romero Leal\, soloist\n_Dark Night of the Soul _(2010) by Ola G
 jeilo (b. 1978) Words by St.\nJohn of the Cross (1542 - 1592)\n     Meh
 er Arora\, soloist\n_Light_ (2009) by Tom Kitt (b. 1974) Lyrics by Brian Y
 orkey (b. 1970)\n     Jaidyn Cugier\, Kailah Valencia\, Jared Berger\, 
 Joseph Temple\,\nsoloists\n     Vince Varvel\, guitar\n     Ben Dick
 e\, percussion\n
DTSTAMP:20261119T182346Z
END:VEVENT
