BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:17042
DTSTART;TZID=America/Chicago:20261023T140000
SEQUENCE:0
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
DTEND;TZID=America/Chicago:20261023T150000
LOCATION:560 Music Center\, 63130
SUMMARY:A Workshop with composer Adolphus Hailstork
CLASS:PUBLIC
DESCRIPTION:Join us for a special performer-composer workshop featuring mus
 ic by\nesteemed composer Adolphus Hailstork and students in the WashU musi
 c\nprogram. Known for a rich musical language that blends lyricism\,\nspir
 itual depth\, and vivid orchestral color\, Hailstork has written\nwidely f
 or chorus\, chamber ensembles\, orchestra\, and solo voice\, often\ndrawin
 g inspiration from African American history\, literature\, and\nfaith trad
 itions. During the workshop\, student performers—including\nGiulia Vitol
 o\, the WashU Chamber Choir\, and several others—will\nshare selected wo
 rks for discussion\, rehearsal insight\, and direct\nfeedback from the com
 poser himself. This collaborative event offers a\nrare opportunity to hear
  Hailstork’s music interpreted by WashU\nmusicians while also learning f
 rom his perspective on style\,\ninterpretation\, and expressive intent. Th
 e workshop will highlight the\nenduring significance of Hailstork’s voic
 e in contemporary American\nmusic.\n\nOpera Theatre of Saint Louis will fe
 ature _Joshua’s Boots\, _an opera\nfor young people by Adolphus Hailstor
 k and Susan Kander from October\n21-24th.  Follow the link to learn more!
 \n\nJoshua's Boots [https://opera-stl.org/whats-on/joshuas-boots/]\n\nPROG
 RAM\n\n_Yuhwa (The Goddess of the Willow Trees) _for solo flute (sophomore
 )\nGiulia Vitolo\, flute \n\n\"My Heart to Thy Heart\" from Four _Romanti
 c Love Songs _(PhD student)\nChristina Smiley\, voice \n\n_Three Smiles f
 or Tracey_ for solo clarinet (Faculty)\nDana Hotle\, clarinet\n\n_Nocturne
  _from _Five Short Choral Works_\nWashU Chamber Choir \n\nBIOGRAPHY\n\nAD
 OLPHUS HAILSTORK (b. April 17\, 1941) is one of America's most\ndistinguis
 hed contemporary composers\, renowned for a body of work that\nspans orche
 stral\, choral\, chamber\, band\, and operatic music. Born in\nRochester\,
  New York\, and raised in Albany\, he began studying piano\,\nviolin\, org
 an\, and voice as a child and demonstrated an early passion\nfor compositi
 on. His musical education included studies with some of\nthe twentieth cen
 tury's most influential teachers\, including Mark Fax\nat Howard Universit
 y\, Vittorio Giannini and David Diamond at the\nManhattan School of Music\
 , Nadia Boulanger at the American\nConservatory in Fontainebleau\, and H. 
 Owen Reed at Michigan State\nUniversity\, where he earned his doctorate in
  composition in 1971.\n\nThroughout a career spanning more than five decad
 es\, Hailstork has\ncreated music that draws upon African American musical
  traditions\nwhile embracing the broader classical canon. His compositions
  often\naddress themes of history\, justice\, and cultural identity\, refl
 ecting\nhis belief that music can speak powerfully to social and human\nco
 ncerns. His works have been performed by leading ensembles including\nthe 
 Philadelphia Orchestra\, the Chicago Symphony Orchestra\, the New\nYork Ph
 ilharmonic\, and the Detroit Symphony Orchestra\, and have been\nchampione
 d by conductors such as James DePreist\, Daniel Barenboim\,\nKurt Masur\, 
 and Lorin Maazel. \n\nAmong Hailstork's most notable compositions are his
  symphonies\,\nconcertos\, choral works\, and operas. Opera audiences know
  him\nespecially for _Joshua's Boots_\, commissioned by Opera Theatre of\n
 Saint Louis and Kansas City Lyric Opera and premiered in 1999\, as well\na
 s _Rise for Freedom_\, inspired by the Underground Railroad. His\ncatalog 
 also includes celebrated works such as _American Guernica_\,\n_Whitman's J
 ourney_\, _Earthrise_\, and _Fanfare on “Amazing\nGrace\,”_ which gain
 ed national attention when it was performed\nduring the 2021 U.S. presiden
 tial inauguration.\n\nIn addition to his accomplishments as a composer\, H
 ailstork has been a\ndedicated educator and advocate for American music. H
 e taught at\nYoungstown State University\, Norfolk State University\, and 
 Old\nDominion University\, where he served as Professor of Music and\nComp
 oser-in-Residence. His contributions have been recognized with\nnumerous h
 onors\, including a Fulbright Fellowship and designation as a\nCultural La
 ureate of the Commonwealth of Virginia. Today\, Hailstork is\ncelebrated a
 s a pioneering voice in American classical music whose\nworks continue to 
 enrich the repertoire and inspire audiences around\nthe world.\n\nThis eve
 nt is supported in part by Washington University in St. Louis\nCenter for 
 the Study of Race\, Ethnicity &amp; Equity
DTSTAMP:20261119T182346Z
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:16996
DTSTART;TZID=America/Chicago:20261009T193000
SEQUENCE:0
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
DTEND;TZID=America/Chicago:20261009T203000
LOCATION:560 Music Center\, 63130
SUMMARY:Chamber Project St. Louis: Trailblazer\, a tribute to Rebecca Clark
 e
CLASS:PUBLIC
DESCRIPTION:Discover the music of a true pioneer. Join us for _TRAILBLAZER_
 \, a\ncaptivating evening celebrating the brilliant\, fiercely original\nc
 hamber music of Rebecca Clarke.\n\nTickets\n[https://www.eventbrite.com/e/
 trailblazer-a-tribute-to-rebecca-clarke-tickets-1994995638659?aff=oddtdtcr
 eator]\n\nWashU students free with WashU ID\n$5 students\n$20 general adm
 ission\n\nABOUT THE PROGRAM\n\nAs both a virtuoso violist and a composer\,
  Clarke smashed 20th-century\nglass ceilings\, writing music of incredible
  emotional depth and\nharmonic color. She has been a huge inspiration to u
 s since the\nbeginning\, and we’re paying her tribute with a feature con
 cert. Her\nsweeping _Piano Trio_ is not to be missed\, and CPSTL founder L
 aura\nReycraft brings the beloved _Viola Sonata_ to life with WashU pianis
 t\nYoung Kim.\n\nPERFORMERS\n\nJane Price\, violin\nLaura Reycraft\, viola
 \nMarta Simidtchieva\, cello\nYoung Kim\, piano\n
DTSTAMP:20261119T182346Z
END:VEVENT
