DTEND;TZID=America/Chicago:20260507T140000
SUMMARY:Maxine Silverstein\, Senior Minor Recital\, voice
CLASS:PUBLIC
DESCRIPTION:PROGRAM\n\nSelections from_ Schwanengesang_ (D. 957) by Franz
  Schubert\n\n* \n_Liebesbotschaft_\n* \n_Der Atlas_\n* \n_Die Stadt_\n* \n
 _Der Doppelgänger_\n* \n_Die Taubenpost_\n\n_Don Quichotte à Dulcinée _
 (M.84) by Maurice Ravel\n\n* \n_Chanson Romanesque_\n* \n_Chanson épique_
 \n* \n_Chanson à boire_\n\nINTERMISSION\n \n“Hai già vinta la causa
 ” from _Le Nozze di Figaro_ (K.492) by_\n_Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart\n\n
 “L’ultima canzone” by Francesco Paolo Tosti\n\nSelections from _Sea 
 Pictures _(Op. 37) by  Edward Elgar\n\n* \n_Sabbath Morning at Sea_\n* \n
 _In Haven_\n* \n_The Swimmer_\n\nBIOGRAPHY\n\nMaxine Silverstein is a seni
 or at Washington University in St. Louis.\nMaxine has a major in mathemati
 cs with a minor in music\, focusing on\nmusic composition and voice. Their
  musical journey began in elementary\nschool\, where they experimented wit
 h the piano and violin before\nsettling on the cello. They discovered thei
 r passion for voice in the\ntheater\, participating in several musicals th
 roughout their middle and\nhigh school years. For the past four years\, th
 ey have been studying\nvoice under Nathan Ruggles\, with a focus on classi
 cal voice. They have\nhad the privilege of performing in masterclasses wit
 h Lawrence\nBrownlee\, Marques Jerrell Ruff\, and Dr. Bethany Worrell. Max
 ine wants\nto extend their gratitude to their family\, friends\, and mento
 rs who\nhave supported their endeavors at WashU.
DTSTAMP:20261119T182346Z
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:16944
DTSTART;TZID=America/Chicago:20260319T190000
SEQUENCE:0
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
DTEND;TZID=America/Chicago:20260319T200000
SUMMARY:Staging Baldwin and Buckley: Why Opera? Why Now?
CLASS:PUBLIC
DESCRIPTION:A performance of the opera _The Tongue and The Lash_ and a roun
 d-table\ndiscussion.\n\nWashU Music\, Center for the Study of Race\, and E
 thnicity &amp; Equity\n(CRE²)\, and Opera Theatre of St. Louis present a perf
 ormance and\nroundtable discussion of _The Tongue &amp; The Lash _by composer 
 Damien\nSneed and librettist Karen Chilton\, commissioned by Opera Theatre
  of\nSaint Louis in 2021. _The Tongue and The Lash _imagines the\nconversa
 tion between James Baldwin and William Buckley immediately\nfollowing the 
 1965 debate. In the opera\, the two men continue to spar\nover the meaning
  of racial freedom in America\, maintaining both their\ndignity and convic
 tions. This opera invites us to reflect on whether\,\nas a nation still gr
 appling with deep divisions\, we can preserve the\nart of civil discourse.
 \n\nA round-table discussion will immediately follow the performance\,\nmo
 derated by Professor Adrienne Davis\n[https://law.washu.edu/directory/prof
 ile/adrienne-davis/]\, William M.\nVan Cleve Professor of Law and Professo
 r of Organizational and\nBehavioral and Leadership\, Olin Business School\
 , WashU. The discussion\nwill feature scholars with expertise in opera and
  the writings of\nJames Baldwin including composer Damien Sneed\n[https://
 www.damiensneed.com/]\, distinguished visiting scholar Dr.\nNaomi André [
 https://music.unc.edu/people/musicfaculty/naomi-andre/]\;\nNicholas Buccol
 a\, Ph.D.\, Dr. Jules K. Whitehill Professor of Humanism\nand Ethics\, Dep
 artment of Government\, Claremont McKenna College\; and\nLauren Eldridge S
 tewart\,\n[https://music.washu.edu/people/lauren-eldridge-stewart] Assista
 nt\nProfessor of Ethnomusicology\, WashU.\n\nFree\, but advanced registrat
 ion encouraged.\n\nREGISTER HERE\n[https://ticketing.opera-stl.org/4846/48
 48?_gl=1*1w7kkv6*_gcl_au*NTE4MTc3OTU0LjE3Njc3MjgxNTc.*_ga*MTMwOTM3NDgyNi4x
 NzY3NzI4MTU4*_ga_2D7WY8XCFX*czE3NjgyNDc3MDEkbzIkZzEkdDE3NjgyNDgwNjAkajYwJG
 wwJGgw]\n\nCast &amp; Creative Team\nMovement Consultant: Olivia Gacka\nMusic 
 Director/Pianist: Jonathan Heaney\nStage Manager: Emma Fletcher\nJames Bal
 dwin: Markel Reed\nWilliam F. Buckley\, Jr.: Andrew Morstein\nAdjudicator:
  Robert Mellon\n\nREAD MORE\n\nSTL American\, “Revisiting Baldwin”\n[h
 ttps://www.stlamerican.com/arts-and-entertainment/living-it-arts-and-enter
 tainment/revisiting-baldwin/] \n\nSTL Mag\, “Short Opera about Baldwin 
 and Buckley makes a triumphant\nSt. Louis return” \n[https://www.stlmag
 .com/culture/the-tongue-and-the-lash-opera-stlouis/]\n\nThe Source\, “Th
 e Tongue and the Lash” \n[https://source.washu.edu/2026/03/the-tongue-a
 nd-the-lash/]\n\n_Header Image Credit: Baldwin and Buckley debate at Cambr
 idge Union\,\nFebruary 18\, 1965_\n\n-------------------------\n\n_This vi
 sit is sponsored in part through funding from the __Office of\nthe Provost
 _\n[https://facultyaffairs.washu.edu/funding-opportunities/distinguished-v
 isiting-scholars-program/]_:\nDistinguished Visiting Scholar Program._\n\n
 _Belonging in Opera_\n[https://cre2.washu.edu/community/arts-culture/race-
 and-opera/]_ is\npresented by the Center for the Study of Race\, Ethnicit
 y &amp; Equity\,\nOpera Theatre of St. Louis\, and Washington University’s D
 epartment\nof Music._\n
DTSTAMP:20261119T182346Z
END:VEVENT
