BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:16959
DTSTART;TZID=America/Chicago:20260318T163000
SEQUENCE:0
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
DTEND;TZID=America/Chicago:20260318T180000
SUMMARY:The Potential of Operatic Spaces Today\, Dr. Naomi André
CLASS:PUBLIC
DESCRIPTION:DATE: March 18\, 2026\n\nTIME: 4:30 PM\n\nLOCATION: Seigle Hall
 \, Room 109\n\nDetails about directions &amp; parking located below.\n\n------
 -------------------\n\nFor many\, the opera house conjures images of a pas
 t glory that has\nfaded in importance and relevance. Yet even though its s
 todginess\nmight seem out of date\, today operatic spaces are being transf
 ormed in\nmultiple ways that speak directly to our current realities. As t
 he\ncanonic repertory is performed in thoughtful and provocative\nproducti
 ons\, the people involved with opera (performers\, directors\,\nadministra
 tors) are expanding the way familiar stories are being told.\nAdditionally
 \, the types of narratives being portrayed include\ninventive and ground-b
 reaking themes and situations. The most\ncompelling opera companies are pr
 esenting traditional operas in novel\nways along with creating paths for i
 nnovative newer works. A key\nelement in opera today is for the audience t
 o see something that\nconnects to who they are and represents a wide range
  of experiences\nand situations. In this talk I explore these themes that 
 set the\ngroundwork for my models of shadow operatic cultures and engaged\
 nmusicology. This presentation will also include discussions of\n_Winnie: 
 The Opera_ (Ndodana-Breen/Wilensky/Vundla) and _Fire Shut Up\nin My Bones_
  (Blanchard/Lemmons). \n\nRSVP Here [https://form.jotform.com/26019705588
 9168]\n \nNAOMI ANDRÉ is the David G. Frey Distinguished Professor in th
 e\nDepartment of Music at the University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill\
 nand Professor _Emerita_ at the University of Michigan in Afroamerican\nan
 d African Studies\, Women’s and Gender Studies\, and the Residential\nCo
 llege. \n\nHer publications include the books _Black Opera: History\, Pow
 er\,\nEngagement_ and _Voicing Gender: Castrati\, Travesti\, and the Secon
 d\nWoman in Early Nineteenth-Century Italian Opera_ and co-edited\ncollect
 ions _Blackness in Opera_\; _African Performance Arts and\nPolitical Acts_
 \; and _The Music of Mzilikazi Khumalo: Language\,\nCulture\, and Song in 
 South Africa_. Actively engaged with performance\ntoday\, she has worked w
 ith opera companies\, symphonies\, and is a\nfounding member of the Black 
 Opera Research Network (BORN). She was\nthe inaugural _Scholar in Residenc
 e_ at the Seattle Opera (2019-2024)\nand has served on the Boards of the A
 merican Musicological Society\,\nthe Kurt Weill Foundation\, and Detroit O
 pera.\n\nShe has served on the Graduate Alumni Council for Harvard\nUniver
 sity’s Graduate School of Art and Sciences\, the Executive\nCommittee fo
 r the Criminal Justice Program at the American Friends\nService Committee 
 (Ann Arbor\, MI)\, the Board of Directors for the\nAmerican Musicological 
 Society\, and is currently on the boards of\nDetroit Opera and the Kurt We
 ill Foundation. From 2019-2024\, Dr.\nAndré served as the inaugural Schol
 ar in Residence at the Seattle\nOpera. In her role\, she advised Seattle O
 pera staff and leadership on\nmatters of race and gender in opera\; consul
 ted in artistic planning as\nit relates to representation of race and gend
 er\; participated in\ncompany panel discussions\, podcast recordings\; and
  contributed essays\nto opera programs.\n\n-------------------------\n\nDI
 RECTIONS &amp; PARKING\n\nThis event will take place in Seigle Hall\n[https://
 happenings.washu.edu/seigle_hall_387]\, located on the\nDanforth Campus of
  Washington University in St. Louis. Once within\nSeigle Hall\, Room 109 i
 s on the first floor.\n\nFor those of you coming from off campus\, we reco
 mmend parking in\nthe Danforth University Center (DUC) Parking Garage\n[h
 ttps://parking.washu.edu/items/danforth-underground-garage/] (You\ncan ac
 cess the DUC Garage by the Wallace and Forsyth intersection\n(northbound))
 . Visitor parking is available on the first level or on\nthe down ramp to 
 the second level. To exit the garage\, take the North\nelevator up and go 
 East towards Graham Chapel. \n\nTo view a map of campus\, click here\n[h
 ttps://washu.edu/app/uploads/2024/09/Danforth-Campus-Map.pdf].\nSeigle Hal
 l is location #166 (C3) on the map. The Danforth University\nCenter Parki
 ng Garage\n[https://parking.washu.edu/items/danforth-underground-garage/]
  is\nlocation #200 (E4) on the map. To view an interactive map of campus
 \,\nclick here\n[https://nam10.safelinks.protection.outlook.com/?url=http
 s%3A%2F%2Fprotect.checkpoint.com%2Fv2%2Fr01%2F___https%3A%2F%2Fwustladm.ma
 ps.arcgis.com%2Fapps%2Fwebappviewer%2Findex.html%3Fid%3D1d935c37546d408ea9
 5e02f9793ef0f8___.YzJ1OnN0bG9wZXJhOmM6bzoxZWYxMzIzODEwYWE4NDJlOTBhOTdiOTZm
 N2I2NWY3Mzo3OjZlNTY6NmE5N2IwY2FjODU2MWNiNGQ4YzA3MmMyYjI2YmYwMGEzZTYwMjhhNj
 djMTA0ZThiYmZmMDQ5MTY2YmM1YjUzNDpoOlQ6Tg&amp;data=05%7C02%7Cg.l.lauriello%40wu
 stl.edu%7Cc73c2631059e4b6350bc08de7bd49ccc%7C4ccca3b571cd4e6d974b4d9beb96c
 6d6%7C0%7C0%7C639084350258313437%7CUnknown%7CTWFpbGZsb3d8eyJFbXB0eU1hcGkiO
 nRydWUsIlYiOiIwLjAuMDAwMCIsIlAiOiJXaW4zMiIsIkFOIjoiTWFpbCIsIldUIjoyfQ%3D%3
 D%7C0%7C%7C%7C&amp;sdata=z%2BsULAzJ8ysNkfC4rS85we%2BjRRKAhMzcB6mEGCsGur0%3D&amp;re
 served=0].\nYou may also search “Graham Memorial Chapel” and “Danfor
 th\nUniversity Center Parking Garage” to find their location.\n\nParking
  is free for visitors after 5:00pm in yellow spaces. \n\n----------------
 ---------\n\n_This visit is sponsored in part through funding from the __O
 ffice of\nthe Provost_\n[https://facultyaffairs.washu.edu/funding-opportun
 ities/distinguished-visiting-scholars-program/]_:\nDistinguished Visiting 
 Scholar Program._\n\n_Belonging in Opera_\n[https://cre2.washu.edu/communi
 ty/arts-culture/race-and-opera/]_ is\npresented by the Center for the Stu
 dy of Race\, Ethnicity &amp; Equity\,\nOpera Theatre of St. Louis\, and Washin
 gton University’s Department\nof Music._\n
DTSTAMP:20261119T182346Z
END:VEVENT
